1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,753
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,933 --> 00:00:04,127
Frugal living has a branding problem.

3
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:07,298
Some people hear frugal and picture someone

4
00:00:07,298 --> 00:00:08,481
reusing tea bags,

5
00:00:08,481 --> 00:00:10,776
arguing over a two dollar coupon,

6
00:00:10,776 --> 00:00:12,515
and never having any fun.

7
00:00:12,695 --> 00:00:15,393
Other people treat frugality like a magic

8
00:00:15,393 --> 00:00:17,037
formula for getting rich.

9
00:00:17,217 --> 00:00:19,028
The truth is somewhere in the middle.

10
00:00:19,208 --> 00:00:22,018
Some frugal habits genuinely build wealth.

11
00:00:22,198 --> 00:00:23,557
Others waste your time,

12
00:00:23,557 --> 00:00:25,449
your health, or even your money.

13
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:28,584
Today, twenty frugal habits that actually make

14
00:00:28,584 --> 00:00:30,447
you rich, and ten that don't.

15
00:00:30,797 --> 00:00:33,362
Here's the idea that separates the two.

16
00:00:33,542 --> 00:00:35,913
Imagine the same five hundred dollars a

17
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:36,642
month saved.

18
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:38,926
If it comes from big decisions like

19
00:00:38,926 --> 00:00:39,949
housing and cars,

20
00:00:39,949 --> 00:00:41,873
it takes almost no daily effort.

21
00:00:42,053 --> 00:00:43,823
If it comes from tiny cuts,

22
00:00:43,823 --> 00:00:46,381
it can take dozens of painful decisions

23
00:00:46,381 --> 00:00:47,103
every week.

24
00:00:47,283 --> 00:00:49,315
Same money, very different life.

25
00:00:49,664 --> 00:00:51,557
That's the key to smart frugality.

26
00:00:51,737 --> 00:00:53,869
It's not about spending as little as

27
00:00:53,869 --> 00:00:55,232
possible on everything.

28
00:00:55,412 --> 00:00:57,694
It's about spending less on things that

29
00:00:57,694 --> 00:00:58,865
don't matter to you,

30
00:00:58,865 --> 00:01:00,680
so you can spend more on things

31
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:02,378
that do, and invest the rest.

32
00:01:02,558 --> 00:01:04,530
Researchers who study wealth,

33
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:05,890
like Thomas Stanley,

34
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:08,678
found that many millionaires were frugal,

35
00:01:08,678 --> 00:01:10,174
but in a targeted way.

36
00:01:10,354 --> 00:01:11,928
They cut hard on cars,

37
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:13,645
houses and status items,

38
00:01:13,645 --> 00:01:16,365
and still spent on things they valued,

39
00:01:16,365 --> 00:01:18,225
like education and family.

40
00:01:18,576 --> 00:01:20,929
We'll go through these thirty habits from

41
00:01:20,929 --> 00:01:21,675
three angles.

42
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:23,156
First, the numbers.

43
00:01:23,336 --> 00:01:25,500
Ten frugal habits with math so strong

44
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,431
they can change your net worth by

45
00:01:27,431 --> 00:01:29,361
hundreds of thousands of dollars.

46
00:01:29,541 --> 00:01:31,608
Second, the psychology.

47
00:01:31,788 --> 00:01:34,439
Ten frugal habits that feel smart but

48
00:01:34,439 --> 00:01:37,161
backfire, and why our brains love them

49
00:01:37,161 --> 00:01:37,662
anyway.

50
00:01:37,842 --> 00:01:39,340
And third, the playbook.

51
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:42,033
Ten habits that turn frugality into an

52
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:44,810
automatic system, so you can stop thinking

53
00:01:44,810 --> 00:01:46,463
about money all the time.

54
00:01:46,813 --> 00:01:48,403
Let's start with the numbers.

55
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:50,825
Habit number one is the single biggest

56
00:01:50,825 --> 00:01:52,890
frugal move most families can make.

57
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:54,123
Our sources.

58
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:56,253
Car costs come from AAA,

59
00:01:56,253 --> 00:01:58,610
Kelley Blue Book and Edmunds.

60
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:01,392
Food spending from the Bureau of Labor

61
00:02:01,392 --> 00:02:02,145
Statistics.

62
00:02:02,325 --> 00:02:04,647
Credit card rates from the Federal Reserve.

63
00:02:04,827 --> 00:02:07,881
Millionaire habits from Thomas Stanley's research.

64
00:02:08,061 --> 00:02:10,639
Health spending research from the Centers for

65
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:12,415
Disease Control and Prevention.

66
00:02:12,595 --> 00:02:15,536
Growth examples use a stated seven percent

67
00:02:15,536 --> 00:02:17,076
average yearly return,

68
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:19,247
an illustration, not a promise.

69
00:02:19,427 --> 00:02:20,713
This is education,

70
00:02:20,713 --> 00:02:22,214
not financial advice.

71
00:02:22,563 --> 00:02:23,318
Angle one.

72
00:02:23,498 --> 00:02:24,264
The numbers.

73
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:27,350
Ten frugal habits that genuinely make people

74
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:29,530
rich, because the math is so big.

75
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:31,040
Habit number one.

76
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:33,960
Buy reliable used cars and drive them

77
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:35,145
for a long time.

78
00:02:35,495 --> 00:02:38,475
AAA estimates that owning and operating a

79
00:02:38,475 --> 00:02:41,310
new car costs more than twelve thousand

80
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:43,200
dollars a year on average,

81
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,672
including depreciation, insurance,

82
00:02:45,672 --> 00:02:47,998
fuel, maintenance and financing.

83
00:02:48,178 --> 00:02:50,713
Kelley Blue Book reported average new car

84
00:02:50,713 --> 00:02:52,939
prices above fifty thousand dollars.

85
00:02:53,119 --> 00:02:54,721
A reliable used car,

86
00:02:54,721 --> 00:02:56,244
kept for ten years,

87
00:02:56,244 --> 00:02:58,889
can cut those costs dramatically.

88
00:02:59,239 --> 00:03:02,336
Stanley found that many millionaires bought used

89
00:03:02,336 --> 00:03:04,208
cars and kept them for years.

90
00:03:04,388 --> 00:03:05,303
Over a lifetime,

91
00:03:05,303 --> 00:03:07,476
the difference between a new car every

92
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:09,134
few years and a used car kept

93
00:03:09,134 --> 00:03:11,364
long can reach hundreds of thousands of

94
00:03:11,364 --> 00:03:13,594
dollars, once you count what that money

95
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:15,138
could have earned invested.

96
00:03:15,489 --> 00:03:16,464
Make it yours.

97
00:03:16,644 --> 00:03:18,470
Next time, buy a car two or

98
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:21,244
three years old with a strong reliability

99
00:03:21,244 --> 00:03:21,717
record.

100
00:03:21,897 --> 00:03:24,508
Keep it until repairs are frequent and

101
00:03:24,508 --> 00:03:25,195
expensive.

102
00:03:25,375 --> 00:03:26,528
When the loan ends,

103
00:03:26,528 --> 00:03:28,532
keep paying yourself the payment.

104
00:03:28,883 --> 00:03:29,939
Habit number two.

105
00:03:30,119 --> 00:03:31,917
Live in a home that fits your

106
00:03:31,917 --> 00:03:33,962
needs, not your maximum approval.

107
00:03:34,312 --> 00:03:36,824
Housing is the biggest expense for most

108
00:03:36,824 --> 00:03:39,980
American households, according to Bureau of Labor

109
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:41,010
Statistics data.

110
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:43,658
A smaller or cheaper home means a

111
00:03:43,658 --> 00:03:46,424
lower mortgage, lower property taxes,

112
00:03:46,424 --> 00:03:50,537
lower insurance, lower utilities and lower maintenance.

113
00:03:50,717 --> 00:03:52,966
It's savings on five bills at once,

114
00:03:52,966 --> 00:03:54,187
every single month,

115
00:03:54,187 --> 00:03:56,115
for as long as you live there.

116
00:03:56,466 --> 00:03:58,524
Buying a home a hundred thousand dollars

117
00:03:58,524 --> 00:04:00,737
cheaper than your maximum approval can save

118
00:04:00,737 --> 00:04:02,178
hundreds of dollars a month.

119
00:04:02,358 --> 00:04:03,604
Invested over decades,

120
00:04:03,604 --> 00:04:05,701
that difference can become a fortune.

121
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:08,132
Many quiet millionaires live in homes far

122
00:04:08,132 --> 00:04:09,724
below what they could afford.

123
00:04:10,075 --> 00:04:11,003
Make it yours.

124
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:13,757
Decide your housing budget before talking to

125
00:04:13,757 --> 00:04:14,283
a lender.

126
00:04:14,463 --> 00:04:17,104
Aim to keep total housing costs around

127
00:04:17,104 --> 00:04:19,397
thirty percent of income or less.

128
00:04:19,577 --> 00:04:21,101
Think about the life you want,

129
00:04:21,101 --> 00:04:22,828
not the house you're approved for.

130
00:04:23,179 --> 00:04:24,177
Habit number three.

131
00:04:24,357 --> 00:04:26,238
Cook most meals at home.

132
00:04:26,587 --> 00:04:28,531
Food away from home is one of

133
00:04:28,531 --> 00:04:32,149
the largest budget categories for American households,

134
00:04:32,149 --> 00:04:35,098
according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics.

135
00:04:35,278 --> 00:04:37,657
A home cooked meal often costs a

136
00:04:37,657 --> 00:04:40,036
fraction of the same meal from a

137
00:04:40,036 --> 00:04:42,044
restaurant or delivery app,

138
00:04:42,044 --> 00:04:45,018
especially once fees and tips are added.

139
00:04:45,368 --> 00:04:48,534
Replacing even three restaurant or delivery meals

140
00:04:48,534 --> 00:04:50,601
a week can save a typical family

141
00:04:50,601 --> 00:04:52,927
well over a thousand dollars a year.

142
00:04:53,107 --> 00:04:55,249
And cooking at home is often healthier,

143
00:04:55,249 --> 00:04:57,062
which saves money in a second way

144
00:04:57,062 --> 00:04:57,611
over time.

145
00:04:57,961 --> 00:04:58,832
Make it yours.

146
00:04:59,012 --> 00:05:01,711
Plan four dinners each weekend and shop

147
00:05:01,711 --> 00:05:02,542
with a list.

148
00:05:02,722 --> 00:05:04,591
Cook double portions for lunches.

149
00:05:04,771 --> 00:05:06,738
Keep a few fast backup meals in

150
00:05:06,738 --> 00:05:08,579
the freezer for tired nights.

151
00:05:08,928 --> 00:05:10,148
Habit number four.

152
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:12,696
Pay off high interest debt aggressively.

153
00:05:13,045 --> 00:05:15,858
The Federal Reserve reports average credit card

154
00:05:15,858 --> 00:05:18,491
rates above twenty percent for accounts that

155
00:05:18,491 --> 00:05:19,268
pay interest.

156
00:05:19,448 --> 00:05:21,488
Paying off a card at twenty two

157
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:24,317
percent is like earning a guaranteed twenty

158
00:05:24,317 --> 00:05:25,567
two percent return.

159
00:05:25,747 --> 00:05:28,092
No investment reliably offers that.

160
00:05:28,442 --> 00:05:30,350
On a ten thousand dollar balance,

161
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:33,010
that's around two thousand two hundred dollars

162
00:05:33,010 --> 00:05:34,108
a year in interest.

163
00:05:34,288 --> 00:05:36,765
Eliminating it frees up that money every

164
00:05:36,765 --> 00:05:37,632
year, forever.

165
00:05:37,812 --> 00:05:40,076
That's frugality with real teeth.

166
00:05:40,425 --> 00:05:41,343
Make it yours.

167
00:05:41,523 --> 00:05:43,276
List debts by interest rate.

168
00:05:43,456 --> 00:05:44,782
Pay minimums on all,

169
00:05:44,782 --> 00:05:47,234
and every extra dollar to the highest

170
00:05:47,234 --> 00:05:47,566
rate.

171
00:05:47,746 --> 00:05:50,295
Stop adding new charges to cards with

172
00:05:50,295 --> 00:05:50,915
balances.

173
00:05:51,265 --> 00:05:52,392
Habit number five.

174
00:05:52,572 --> 00:05:54,139
Keep investment fees low.

175
00:05:54,488 --> 00:05:56,822
A one percent yearly fee sounds small.

176
00:05:57,002 --> 00:05:59,097
But on a growing retirement account,

177
00:05:59,097 --> 00:06:00,959
it can cost tens of thousands of

178
00:06:00,959 --> 00:06:02,705
dollars or more over a career,

179
00:06:02,705 --> 00:06:04,567
because you lose the fee and all

180
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:06,545
the growth it would have produced.

181
00:06:06,725 --> 00:06:09,271
Many broad index funds charge a small

182
00:06:09,271 --> 00:06:10,441
fraction of that.

183
00:06:10,790 --> 00:06:12,523
This is one of the few frugal

184
00:06:12,523 --> 00:06:14,673
habits that takes about an hour once

185
00:06:14,673 --> 00:06:16,166
and pays off for decades.

186
00:06:16,346 --> 00:06:17,752
You don't have to think about it

187
00:06:17,752 --> 00:06:18,192
every day.

188
00:06:18,372 --> 00:06:20,650
You just choose low cost funds and

189
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:21,588
let time work.

190
00:06:21,937 --> 00:06:22,843
Make it yours.

191
00:06:23,023 --> 00:06:25,427
Look up the expense ratio of every

192
00:06:25,427 --> 00:06:27,760
fund in your retirement accounts.

193
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:30,591
Compare with low cost alternatives in the

194
00:06:30,591 --> 00:06:31,237
same plan.

195
00:06:31,417 --> 00:06:32,985
If you use an advisor,

196
00:06:32,985 --> 00:06:35,550
ask exactly what you pay in dollars.

197
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:37,224
Habit number six.

198
00:06:37,404 --> 00:06:39,471
Shop for insurance once a year.

199
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:42,776
Car and home insurance premiums have risen

200
00:06:42,776 --> 00:06:44,464
sharply in recent years.

201
00:06:44,644 --> 00:06:47,576
Many people renew automatically for years without

202
00:06:47,576 --> 00:06:48,174
comparing.

203
00:06:48,354 --> 00:06:50,165
Getting a few quotes once a year

204
00:06:50,165 --> 00:06:52,316
can often save hundreds of dollars for

205
00:06:52,316 --> 00:06:53,335
the same coverage.

206
00:06:53,684 --> 00:06:55,403
It's an ideal frugal habit.

207
00:06:55,583 --> 00:06:56,732
One hour a year,

208
00:06:56,732 --> 00:06:58,385
no change in lifestyle,

209
00:06:58,385 --> 00:07:00,540
and often a meaningful saving.

210
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:02,321
Raising your deductible,

211
00:07:02,321 --> 00:07:04,655
if you have a solid emergency fund,

212
00:07:04,655 --> 00:07:06,456
can lower premiums further.

213
00:07:06,805 --> 00:07:07,676
Make it yours.

214
00:07:07,856 --> 00:07:10,383
Put an annual reminder in your calendar

215
00:07:10,383 --> 00:07:11,873
a month before renewal.

216
00:07:12,053 --> 00:07:13,887
Get at least three quotes for the

217
00:07:13,887 --> 00:07:14,665
same coverage.

218
00:07:14,845 --> 00:07:17,202
Ask about bundling and discounts.

219
00:07:17,552 --> 00:07:18,702
Habit number seven.

220
00:07:18,882 --> 00:07:21,088
Cancel subscriptions you don't use.

221
00:07:21,437 --> 00:07:25,095
Streaming, apps, memberships and boxes add up.

222
00:07:25,275 --> 00:07:28,481
Surveys regularly find people underestimate what they

223
00:07:28,481 --> 00:07:29,872
spend on subscriptions.

224
00:07:30,052 --> 00:07:31,979
Each one renews automatically,

225
00:07:31,979 --> 00:07:34,290
so the cost continues long after the

226
00:07:34,290 --> 00:07:35,253
interest fades.

227
00:07:35,603 --> 00:07:37,907
A sweep every few months can easily

228
00:07:37,907 --> 00:07:40,145
free up fifty to a hundred dollars

229
00:07:40,145 --> 00:07:41,988
a month for many households,

230
00:07:41,988 --> 00:07:43,173
with no real loss.

231
00:07:43,353 --> 00:07:46,001
You can always resubscribe when there's something

232
00:07:46,001 --> 00:07:47,730
you really want to watch or use.

233
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:49,056
Make it yours.

234
00:07:49,236 --> 00:07:51,964
Search your statements for every repeating charge.

235
00:07:52,144 --> 00:07:54,191
Cancel anything you haven't used in the

236
00:07:54,191 --> 00:07:54,768
last month.

237
00:07:54,948 --> 00:07:57,975
Rotate streaming services instead of keeping them

238
00:07:57,975 --> 00:07:58,222
all.

239
00:07:58,571 --> 00:07:59,593
Habit number eight.

240
00:07:59,773 --> 00:08:02,014
Always take the full employer match.

241
00:08:02,363 --> 00:08:04,914
Many employers match part of what you

242
00:08:04,914 --> 00:08:06,775
put into a retirement plan.

243
00:08:06,955 --> 00:08:09,412
If your employer matches fifty cents on

244
00:08:09,412 --> 00:08:11,365
the dollar up to six percent of

245
00:08:11,365 --> 00:08:14,325
your salary, contributing less than six percent

246
00:08:14,325 --> 00:08:16,278
leaves free money on the table.

247
00:08:16,628 --> 00:08:18,366
It's technically saving,

248
00:08:18,366 --> 00:08:19,380
not frugality.

249
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,378
But it's the most frugal thing you

250
00:08:21,378 --> 00:08:23,464
can do, because it's the highest return

251
00:08:23,464 --> 00:08:25,121
on any dollar you'll ever save.

252
00:08:25,301 --> 00:08:27,712
Skipping it is like declining part of

253
00:08:27,712 --> 00:08:28,494
your salary.

254
00:08:28,843 --> 00:08:29,691
Make it yours.

255
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:32,366
Check your benefits portal or ask HR

256
00:08:32,366 --> 00:08:34,376
exactly how your match works.

257
00:08:34,556 --> 00:08:36,726
Make sure your contribution is at least

258
00:08:36,726 --> 00:08:38,340
high enough to get all of it.

259
00:08:38,690 --> 00:08:39,805
Habit number nine.

260
00:08:39,985 --> 00:08:42,064
Keep your emergency fund in a high

261
00:08:42,064 --> 00:08:43,410
yield savings account.

262
00:08:43,759 --> 00:08:46,666
Many traditional savings accounts pay a tiny

263
00:08:46,666 --> 00:08:47,591
interest rate.

264
00:08:47,771 --> 00:08:50,099
Online high yield savings accounts,

265
00:08:50,099 --> 00:08:51,429
insured by the FDIC,

266
00:08:51,429 --> 00:08:54,023
have offered rates many times higher in

267
00:08:54,023 --> 00:08:54,888
recent years.

268
00:08:55,068 --> 00:08:57,348
On a ten thousand dollar emergency fund,

269
00:08:57,348 --> 00:08:59,742
that difference can be hundreds of dollars

270
00:08:59,742 --> 00:09:00,141
a year.

271
00:09:00,491 --> 00:09:02,912
It's a fifteen minute change that pays

272
00:09:02,912 --> 00:09:03,931
you every month,

273
00:09:03,931 --> 00:09:05,842
with no extra risk if you stay

274
00:09:05,842 --> 00:09:07,690
within FDIC insurance limits.

275
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:10,877
Frugality isn't only about spending less.

276
00:09:11,057 --> 00:09:13,106
It's also about making your money work

277
00:09:13,106 --> 00:09:13,483
harder.

278
00:09:13,833 --> 00:09:14,739
Make it yours.

279
00:09:14,919 --> 00:09:16,637
Check your current savings rate.

280
00:09:16,817 --> 00:09:19,210
Compare with a few FDIC insured high

281
00:09:19,210 --> 00:09:20,207
yield accounts.

282
00:09:20,387 --> 00:09:22,696
Move your emergency fund if the gap

283
00:09:22,696 --> 00:09:23,290
is large.

284
00:09:23,639 --> 00:09:24,766
Habit number ten.

285
00:09:24,946 --> 00:09:27,137
Pay for quality on things you use

286
00:09:27,137 --> 00:09:27,802
every day.

287
00:09:28,151 --> 00:09:31,460
Smart frugality isn't always buying the cheapest.

288
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:33,702
For items you use constantly,

289
00:09:33,702 --> 00:09:34,840
like a mattress,

290
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,258
work shoes, or a good winter coat,

291
00:09:37,258 --> 00:09:39,605
quality can be cheaper over time.

292
00:09:39,785 --> 00:09:42,418
Cheap versions that wear out quickly cost

293
00:09:42,418 --> 00:09:43,767
more in the long run.

294
00:09:44,116 --> 00:09:46,706
The writer Terry Pratchett described this as

295
00:09:46,706 --> 00:09:48,354
the boots theory of poverty.

296
00:09:48,534 --> 00:09:50,479
A cheap pair of boots that falls

297
00:09:50,479 --> 00:09:52,729
apart every year ends up costing more

298
00:09:52,729 --> 00:09:54,674
than a well made pair that lasts

299
00:09:54,674 --> 00:09:55,221
a decade.

300
00:09:55,401 --> 00:09:57,027
The key word is daily.

301
00:09:57,376 --> 00:09:58,259
Make it yours.

302
00:09:58,439 --> 00:10:00,471
List the five things you use most.

303
00:10:00,651 --> 00:10:03,925
For those, research quality and durability.

304
00:10:04,105 --> 00:10:05,886
For things you use rarely,

305
00:10:05,886 --> 00:10:06,844
cheap is fine.

306
00:10:07,194 --> 00:10:09,202
Here's what the first ten habits can

307
00:10:09,202 --> 00:10:09,760
add up to.

308
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:12,050
If they free up six hundred dollars

309
00:10:12,050 --> 00:10:14,220
a month, invested at a seven percent

310
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:15,546
average yearly return,

311
00:10:15,546 --> 00:10:18,138
after twenty five years that's roughly four

312
00:10:18,138 --> 00:10:20,308
hundred eighty six thousand dollars.

313
00:10:20,657 --> 00:10:21,644
That's angle one.

314
00:10:21,824 --> 00:10:24,416
Ten habits, almost all of them about

315
00:10:24,416 --> 00:10:26,719
big decisions you make once or a

316
00:10:26,719 --> 00:10:27,943
few times a year.

317
00:10:28,123 --> 00:10:29,284
Now angle two.

318
00:10:29,464 --> 00:10:30,520
The psychology.

319
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:33,083
Ten frugal habits that feel smart,

320
00:10:33,083 --> 00:10:34,555
but quietly cost you.

321
00:10:34,904 --> 00:10:37,155
Why do people fall for bad frugal

322
00:10:37,155 --> 00:10:37,633
habits?

323
00:10:37,813 --> 00:10:40,169
Because saving money feels good,

324
00:10:40,169 --> 00:10:42,451
even when the saving is tiny or

325
00:10:42,451 --> 00:10:43,850
the cost is hidden.

326
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:46,135
Our brains love a visible win,

327
00:10:46,135 --> 00:10:48,379
like a coupon or a sale sticker,

328
00:10:48,379 --> 00:10:50,274
and ignore invisible costs,

329
00:10:50,274 --> 00:10:51,256
like our time,

330
00:10:51,256 --> 00:10:53,501
our health, or the money we lose

331
00:10:53,501 --> 00:10:53,922
later.

332
00:10:54,102 --> 00:10:56,354
These next ten are frugal traps.

333
00:10:56,703 --> 00:10:57,992
Trap number one.

334
00:10:58,172 --> 00:11:00,200
Driving across town to save a few

335
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:00,691
dollars.

336
00:11:01,041 --> 00:11:02,634
Gas, wear on your car,

337
00:11:02,634 --> 00:11:04,733
and your time all cost money.

338
00:11:04,913 --> 00:11:07,054
The IRS standard mileage rate,

339
00:11:07,054 --> 00:11:09,980
which estimates the full cost of driving,

340
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:12,549
has been around seventy cents a mile

341
00:11:12,549 --> 00:11:13,691
in recent years.

342
00:11:13,871 --> 00:11:15,916
A twenty mile round trip to save

343
00:11:15,916 --> 00:11:18,346
five dollars can easily cost more than

344
00:11:18,346 --> 00:11:18,921
you save.

345
00:11:19,270 --> 00:11:21,496
The brain sees the five dollar saving

346
00:11:21,496 --> 00:11:23,662
clearly, and the driving cost not at

347
00:11:23,662 --> 00:11:23,903
all.

348
00:11:24,083 --> 00:11:26,188
That's why this trap is so common,

349
00:11:26,188 --> 00:11:28,293
and why so many people feel frugal

350
00:11:28,293 --> 00:11:30,027
while actually losing money.

351
00:11:30,377 --> 00:11:31,086
Avoid it.

352
00:11:31,266 --> 00:11:33,092
Before going out of your way for

353
00:11:33,092 --> 00:11:35,090
a deal, multiply the extra miles by

354
00:11:35,090 --> 00:11:36,288
roughly the IRS rate,

355
00:11:36,288 --> 00:11:38,057
and add the value of your time.

356
00:11:38,237 --> 00:11:40,095
Only go if the saving is clearly

357
00:11:40,095 --> 00:11:40,501
bigger.

358
00:11:40,851 --> 00:11:42,268
Trap number two.

359
00:11:42,448 --> 00:11:44,095
Buying in bulk things you end up

360
00:11:44,095 --> 00:11:44,816
throwing away.

361
00:11:45,166 --> 00:11:47,872
Warehouse clubs can save money on things

362
00:11:47,872 --> 00:11:49,022
you use reliably,

363
00:11:49,022 --> 00:11:50,983
like paper towels or staples.

364
00:11:51,163 --> 00:11:54,389
But the US Department of Agriculture estimates

365
00:11:54,389 --> 00:11:56,844
thirty to forty percent of the food

366
00:11:56,844 --> 00:11:58,036
supply is wasted,

367
00:11:58,036 --> 00:12:00,351
and bulk produce that spoils is a

368
00:12:00,351 --> 00:12:01,403
common culprit.

369
00:12:01,753 --> 00:12:04,377
Bulk packs also make people use more.

370
00:12:04,557 --> 00:12:07,395
A giant bag of snacks disappears faster

371
00:12:07,395 --> 00:12:08,632
than a small one.

372
00:12:08,812 --> 00:12:10,445
The unit price looks lower,

373
00:12:10,445 --> 00:12:12,319
but the total spending goes up.

374
00:12:12,668 --> 00:12:13,307
Avoid it.

375
00:12:13,487 --> 00:12:16,002
Buy bulk only for nonperishables you use

376
00:12:16,002 --> 00:12:16,819
consistently.

377
00:12:16,999 --> 00:12:20,152
For food, buy what you'll realistically eat

378
00:12:20,152 --> 00:12:21,399
before it spoils.

379
00:12:21,749 --> 00:12:23,050
Trap number three.

380
00:12:23,230 --> 00:12:25,992
Skipping checkups and dental cleanings to save

381
00:12:25,992 --> 00:12:26,353
money.

382
00:12:26,702 --> 00:12:29,324
Preventive care often costs far less than

383
00:12:29,324 --> 00:12:30,859
treating problems later.

384
00:12:31,039 --> 00:12:33,496
A dental cleaning is much cheaper than

385
00:12:33,496 --> 00:12:34,336
a root canal.

386
00:12:34,516 --> 00:12:37,476
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention

387
00:12:37,476 --> 00:12:39,985
notes that many chronic diseases can be

388
00:12:39,985 --> 00:12:42,945
prevented or managed better when caught early.

389
00:12:43,294 --> 00:12:47,012
Many insurance plans cover certain preventive services

390
00:12:47,012 --> 00:12:49,560
at no extra cost under the Affordable

391
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:50,179
Care Act.

392
00:12:50,359 --> 00:12:52,947
Skipping them saves nothing and can cost

393
00:12:52,947 --> 00:12:53,981
thousands later.

394
00:12:54,331 --> 00:12:55,086
Avoid it.

395
00:12:55,266 --> 00:12:58,568
Check which preventive services your insurance covers

396
00:12:58,568 --> 00:12:59,253
at no cost,

397
00:12:59,253 --> 00:13:00,374
and schedule them.

398
00:13:00,554 --> 00:13:03,182
Health is the asset that protects every

399
00:13:03,182 --> 00:13:03,991
other asset.

400
00:13:04,341 --> 00:13:05,584
Trap number four.

401
00:13:05,764 --> 00:13:07,865
Saving money on safety equipment.

402
00:13:08,215 --> 00:13:10,508
Tires, brakes, car seats,

403
00:13:10,508 --> 00:13:12,801
smoke detectors, helmets.

404
00:13:12,981 --> 00:13:15,674
These are places where the cheapest option,

405
00:13:15,674 --> 00:13:17,176
or delaying replacement,

406
00:13:17,176 --> 00:13:18,241
can be dangerous.

407
00:13:18,421 --> 00:13:20,758
Worn tires take longer to stop,

408
00:13:20,758 --> 00:13:22,568
especially on wet roads,

409
00:13:22,568 --> 00:13:25,131
according to safety organizations.

410
00:13:25,481 --> 00:13:28,091
An accident costs far more than good

411
00:13:28,091 --> 00:13:30,265
tires, in money and in health.

412
00:13:30,445 --> 00:13:32,900
Frugality should never put you or your

413
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:33,870
family at risk.

414
00:13:34,219 --> 00:13:34,846
Avoid it.

415
00:13:35,026 --> 00:13:37,685
Spend appropriately on safety.

416
00:13:37,865 --> 00:13:39,982
Replace tires when they're worn,

417
00:13:39,982 --> 00:13:41,504
keep brakes maintained,

418
00:13:41,504 --> 00:13:44,018
and buy safety gear that meets current

419
00:13:44,018 --> 00:13:44,680
standards.

420
00:13:45,030 --> 00:13:46,424
Trap number five.

421
00:13:46,604 --> 00:13:48,995
Buying the cheapest version every time.

422
00:13:49,345 --> 00:13:50,843
Remember the boots theory.

423
00:13:51,023 --> 00:13:53,288
Cheap items that break quickly have to

424
00:13:53,288 --> 00:13:55,017
be replaced, again and again.

425
00:13:55,197 --> 00:13:57,301
The total cost over years can far

426
00:13:57,301 --> 00:13:59,597
exceed a better version bought once.

427
00:13:59,947 --> 00:14:02,129
This trap is common with tools,

428
00:14:02,129 --> 00:14:04,591
appliances, furniture and clothing.

429
00:14:04,771 --> 00:14:06,554
The low price feels like a win

430
00:14:06,554 --> 00:14:09,049
at checkout, but the replacement costs are

431
00:14:09,049 --> 00:14:10,832
spread out and easy to ignore.

432
00:14:11,181 --> 00:14:11,925
Avoid it.

433
00:14:12,105 --> 00:14:14,636
For anything you'll use often or for

434
00:14:14,636 --> 00:14:17,941
years, check reviews for durability and compare

435
00:14:17,941 --> 00:14:19,418
cost per year of use,

436
00:14:19,418 --> 00:14:20,754
not just the price.

437
00:14:21,103 --> 00:14:22,009
Quick break.

438
00:14:22,189 --> 00:14:23,619
If you're enjoying this,

439
00:14:23,619 --> 00:14:25,823
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

440
00:14:26,003 --> 00:14:27,744
It's free and it helps the channel

441
00:14:27,744 --> 00:14:28,000
grow.

442
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:30,862
Okay, frugal trap number six.

443
00:14:31,042 --> 00:14:32,923
This one hurts more than your wallet.

444
00:14:33,272 --> 00:14:34,724
Trap number six.

445
00:14:34,904 --> 00:14:37,737
Cutting all spending on friends and fun.

446
00:14:38,086 --> 00:14:40,421
Some people get so focused on saving

447
00:14:40,421 --> 00:14:42,366
that they stop seeing friends,

448
00:14:42,366 --> 00:14:43,468
skip every event,

449
00:14:43,468 --> 00:14:44,960
and cut every pleasure.

450
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:48,490
Research has linked loneliness and social isolation

451
00:14:48,490 --> 00:14:50,132
to worse health outcomes.

452
00:14:50,312 --> 00:14:53,098
And extreme budgets often snap back into

453
00:14:53,098 --> 00:14:54,491
big spending binges.

454
00:14:54,841 --> 00:14:57,941
Relationships are also a financial asset.

455
00:14:58,121 --> 00:14:59,677
Friends help with jobs,

456
00:14:59,677 --> 00:15:02,382
opportunities and support in hard times.

457
00:15:02,562 --> 00:15:04,316
Cutting them off to save money is

458
00:15:04,316 --> 00:15:04,954
a bad trade.

459
00:15:05,303 --> 00:15:05,988
Avoid it.

460
00:15:06,168 --> 00:15:08,537
Budget for social life on purpose.

461
00:15:08,717 --> 00:15:10,159
Suggest cheaper plans,

462
00:15:10,159 --> 00:15:11,667
like potlucks or hikes,

463
00:15:11,667 --> 00:15:12,978
but keep showing up.

464
00:15:13,327 --> 00:15:14,756
Trap number seven.

465
00:15:14,936 --> 00:15:17,188
Spending hours to save a few dollars.

466
00:15:17,538 --> 00:15:20,306
Extreme couponing can take many hours a

467
00:15:20,306 --> 00:15:20,661
week.

468
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:22,985
If those hours save fifty dollars,

469
00:15:22,985 --> 00:15:25,130
that might be less than you'd earn

470
00:15:25,130 --> 00:15:26,707
in the same time at work,

471
00:15:26,707 --> 00:15:27,401
a side job,

472
00:15:27,401 --> 00:15:29,546
or by building a skill that raises

473
00:15:29,546 --> 00:15:30,303
your income.

474
00:15:30,653 --> 00:15:32,993
Coupons also push people to buy things

475
00:15:32,993 --> 00:15:33,978
they don't need,

476
00:15:33,978 --> 00:15:35,703
just because there's a deal.

477
00:15:35,883 --> 00:15:37,009
The saving is real,

478
00:15:37,009 --> 00:15:38,727
but so is the extra spending.

479
00:15:39,078 --> 00:15:39,739
Avoid it.

480
00:15:39,919 --> 00:15:42,055
Use coupons and cash back apps for

481
00:15:42,055 --> 00:15:43,500
things you already buy,

482
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:44,505
when it's quick.

483
00:15:44,685 --> 00:15:46,669
Spend most of your energy on the

484
00:15:46,669 --> 00:15:48,157
big wins from angle one.

485
00:15:48,506 --> 00:15:49,888
Trap number eight.

486
00:15:50,068 --> 00:15:52,901
Dropping important insurance to save money.

487
00:15:53,250 --> 00:15:55,556
Health, renters, homeowners,

488
00:15:55,556 --> 00:15:58,027
car liability, disability and,

489
00:15:58,027 --> 00:16:00,003
if others depend on you,

490
00:16:00,003 --> 00:16:01,238
life insurance.

491
00:16:01,418 --> 00:16:04,044
Skipping these saves money every month,

492
00:16:04,044 --> 00:16:06,805
until the one month something goes wrong.

493
00:16:06,985 --> 00:16:09,583
Medical bills are a leading cause of

494
00:16:09,583 --> 00:16:11,316
debt for many Americans.

495
00:16:11,665 --> 00:16:13,956
The frugal move is to insure against

496
00:16:13,956 --> 00:16:15,864
disasters you couldn't afford,

497
00:16:15,864 --> 00:16:18,409
while self insuring small losses with an

498
00:16:18,409 --> 00:16:19,363
emergency fund.

499
00:16:19,543 --> 00:16:21,951
That's very different from having no insurance

500
00:16:21,951 --> 00:16:22,318
at all.

501
00:16:22,667 --> 00:16:23,283
Avoid it.

502
00:16:23,463 --> 00:16:25,576
Keep coverage for catastrophes.

503
00:16:25,756 --> 00:16:28,883
Save money by raising deductibles and shopping

504
00:16:28,883 --> 00:16:31,874
around, not by dropping coverage completely.

505
00:16:32,224 --> 00:16:33,502
Trap number nine.

506
00:16:33,682 --> 00:16:35,455
Doing every job yourself,

507
00:16:35,455 --> 00:16:36,874
even the risky ones.

508
00:16:37,224 --> 00:16:39,694
Doing simple repairs yourself can save a

509
00:16:39,694 --> 00:16:39,941
lot.

510
00:16:40,121 --> 00:16:41,516
But electrical work,

511
00:16:41,516 --> 00:16:44,237
gas lines, major plumbing and roof work

512
00:16:44,237 --> 00:16:45,423
can be dangerous,

513
00:16:45,423 --> 00:16:48,004
and mistakes can be very expensive to

514
00:16:48,004 --> 00:16:48,283
fix.

515
00:16:48,463 --> 00:16:50,495
Some work also needs permits.

516
00:16:50,844 --> 00:16:53,218
A failed DIY project can cost more

517
00:16:53,218 --> 00:16:55,940
than hiring a professional in the first

518
00:16:55,940 --> 00:16:56,359
place.

519
00:16:56,539 --> 00:16:58,557
And your safety is worth more than

520
00:16:58,557 --> 00:16:59,210
any saving.

521
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:00,128
Avoid it.

522
00:17:00,308 --> 00:17:03,048
Do simple, low risk jobs yourself.

523
00:17:03,228 --> 00:17:05,504
For anything dangerous or complex,

524
00:17:05,504 --> 00:17:08,650
get several quotes from licensed professionals.

525
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,394
Trap number ten.

526
00:17:10,574 --> 00:17:12,745
Buying things because they're on sale.

527
00:17:13,095 --> 00:17:15,805
Saving forty percent on something you didn't

528
00:17:15,805 --> 00:17:16,914
need isn't saving.

529
00:17:17,094 --> 00:17:18,905
It's spending sixty percent.

530
00:17:19,085 --> 00:17:21,432
Retailers know that sale signs trigger a

531
00:17:21,432 --> 00:17:22,547
feeling of winning,

532
00:17:22,547 --> 00:17:24,542
and they use it to move inventory.

533
00:17:24,892 --> 00:17:27,971
Sales, flash deals and limited time offers

534
00:17:27,971 --> 00:17:29,071
create urgency.

535
00:17:29,251 --> 00:17:30,456
Under that pressure,

536
00:17:30,456 --> 00:17:32,685
people buy things they'd never buy at

537
00:17:32,685 --> 00:17:34,553
full price, and many of them go

538
00:17:34,553 --> 00:17:34,975
unused.

539
00:17:35,325 --> 00:17:36,010
Avoid it.

540
00:17:36,190 --> 00:17:38,008
Ask: would I buy this at full

541
00:17:38,008 --> 00:17:38,384
price?

542
00:17:38,564 --> 00:17:39,969
If not, skip it.

543
00:17:40,149 --> 00:17:42,175
Keep a list of things you actually

544
00:17:42,175 --> 00:17:44,142
need, and only buy those on sale.

545
00:17:44,493 --> 00:17:46,706
Why do the bad habits feel so

546
00:17:46,706 --> 00:17:47,546
satisfying?

547
00:17:47,726 --> 00:17:49,713
Because they give you a visible,

548
00:17:49,713 --> 00:17:50,582
immediate win.

549
00:17:50,762 --> 00:17:52,685
A coupon, a sale price,

550
00:17:52,685 --> 00:17:54,524
a bigger bulk package.

551
00:17:54,704 --> 00:17:56,619
Your brain gets a little reward.

552
00:17:56,799 --> 00:17:59,070
Meanwhile, the costs are hidden,

553
00:17:59,070 --> 00:18:00,631
delayed or spread out.

554
00:18:00,811 --> 00:18:01,972
Your time, gas,

555
00:18:01,972 --> 00:18:04,758
health and future replacement costs.

556
00:18:05,108 --> 00:18:07,801
Good frugality often feels less exciting.

557
00:18:07,981 --> 00:18:10,715
Buying a used car or shopping insurance

558
00:18:10,715 --> 00:18:12,817
once a year doesn't give you a

559
00:18:12,817 --> 00:18:13,658
weekly rush.

560
00:18:13,838 --> 00:18:16,021
But the savings are huge and permanent.

561
00:18:16,201 --> 00:18:18,471
The trick is learning to value the

562
00:18:18,471 --> 00:18:20,607
big, boring wins over the small,

563
00:18:20,607 --> 00:18:21,542
exciting ones.

564
00:18:21,892 --> 00:18:23,285
Here's a simple test.

565
00:18:23,465 --> 00:18:24,879
For any frugal habit,

566
00:18:24,879 --> 00:18:27,034
ask: how much does this save per

567
00:18:27,034 --> 00:18:29,188
year, and how many hours does it

568
00:18:29,188 --> 00:18:29,525
take?

569
00:18:29,705 --> 00:18:31,126
If the answer is a lot of

570
00:18:31,126 --> 00:18:32,490
money for a little time,

571
00:18:32,490 --> 00:18:32,944
keep it.

572
00:18:33,124 --> 00:18:34,924
If it's a little money for a

573
